UI workflow — Hausa
A. Super Admin console
Require fresh AAL2 MFA.
Pick an existing Mainnet Contributor; show only the minimum verified identity details.
Select the fixed role Contributor Admin (not a free-form role selector).
Choose a specific approved permission profile, expiry date and written reason.
Create a one-time invitation; display token/link once and warn not to forward publicly.
Review application and consent versions; view selfie only through audited, short-lived private access.
Record decision + reason. Separate identity review from actual role grant.
Grant or reject explicitly. Revoke at any time; revocation disables access immediately.
B. Nominee flow
Open invitation while signed into the correct ALBUKHR identity.
Server verifies invitation status, expiry, single-use state and Pi identity.
Show purpose, what the role can access, what it cannot access, selfie use, retention and deletion/contact policy.
Obtain affirmative consent before opening camera.
Request camera access with getUserMedia; show live preview.
Capture frame from the live video stream. Do not show gallery/file-picker input.
Allow retake before submit; do not keep local copies longer than necessary.
Upload via controlled private endpoint/storage path; validate size, MIME, image decode and ownership server-side.
Submit application. Show “Pending review”; do not show admin dashboard or permissions yet.
C. Privacy and camera caveats
Camera-only capture is a UI constraint, not proof against spoofing, virtual cameras or manipulated streams.
If liveness is a requirement, conduct a separate vendor/privacy/security assessment and explicit consent review.
Avoid facial recognition/biometric matching unless there is a documented lawful basis, necessity, proportionality and specialist review.
Set a clear retention period; auto-delete rejected/expired photos after approved period; support deletion requests as policy allows.
Private bucket only; signed URLs short-lived; never public URLs.
Do not place selfie in audit log. Audit event should record who accessed it, why, when, and the object reference hash/path only.
